# Rust build output.
/target
**/target/

# Measurement runs write here; the numbers they produce are quoted in
# docs/VALIDATION.md only after they are reproduced by a named suite.
/validation-results/

# Scratch space for ad-hoc runs, including the clean-environment validation,
# which installs into a prefix and protects trees under this directory.
/tmp/

# Release artifacts: the binaries and checksum files `release.yml` assembles.
/dist/

# Python bytecode caches. The scanner never walks these — `__pycache__` is in
# the built-in exclusion list — and one once got committed by accident.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Vim swap files.
*.swp

# ... which collides with the name of this tool's own store, `.swp/`, so the
# store is re-included and then split by half: a project's public identity
# document and release records are meant to be committed alongside the source
# they describe, and only the private half — the root secret, the manifests
# naming every embedded site — is kept out of the history.
!/.swp/
.swp/private/

# The Python binding's own build output. `**/target/` above already covers
# bindings/python/target, so what is listed here is everything maturin and pytest
# write *beside* it: the wheel directory, the interpreter environments the suite
# runs under, and their caches. A built extension in the history is what
# `check-release.sh` fails a release on, so this list is not tidiness.
/bindings/*/dist/
/bindings/*/.venv*/
/bindings/*/.maturin/
/bindings/**/.pytest_cache/
/bindings/**/*.pyd
/bindings/**/*.so
/bindings/**/*.dylib

# The Node binding's own build output: the compiled addon and the package
# manager's install tree. `**/target/` above already covers bindings/node/
# target. Note the asymmetry this file has to carry: `check-release.sh`'s
# artifact sweep matches .pyc/.so/.dll/.dylib/.wasm/.exe but *not* `.node`,
# so the ignore rule below — never committing a built addon — is the control
# that keeps a compiled binary out of the history, and widening the sweep is
# a separate decision for the owner.
#
# `binding.cjs` and `binding.d.ts` are `napi build`'s output beside the addon:
# the loader that selects the platform binary, and the declarations typegen
# reads off the Rust surface. Both are rewritten by every build and by nothing
# else — the entry point callers load, `index.js`, and the types `package.json`
# advertises, `index.d.ts`, are hand-written and tracked. The `files` list in
# package.json ships all four, so ignoring the generated pair costs a published
# package nothing.
/bindings/*/node_modules/
/bindings/**/*.node
/bindings/*/binding.cjs
/bindings/*/binding.d.ts
